r=3D"c8babe" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #c8b=
abe;" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"701" height=3D"399" src=3D"https://toward=
sdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1gJkZbOLK3EM1wFtrNPrl0A.png" al=
t=3D"Image by author" class=3D"wp-image-140947 has-transparency"><figcaptio=
n class=3D"wp-element-caption">Image by author</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">In <a href=3D"https://medium.com/p/2520a34a=
502">the Part 1 article</a>, we learned the gradient boosting regression al=
gorithm in its detail. As we reviewed in that post, the algorithm is flexib=
le enough to deal with any loss functions as long as it is differentiable. =
That means if we just replace the loss function used for regression, specif=
ically mean squared loss, with a loss function that deals with classificati=
on problems, we can perform classification without changing the algorithm i=
tself. Even though the base algorithm is the same, there are still some dif=
ferences that we want to know. In this post, we will dive into all the deta=
ils of the classification algorithm.</p>
<h2 class=3D"wp-block-heading">Algorithm with an Example</h2>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">Gradient boosting is one of the variants of=
 ensemble methods where you create multiple weak models (they are often dec=
ision trees) and combine them to get better performance as a whole. In this=
 section, we are building a gradient boosting classification model using ve=
ry simple example data to intuitively understand how it works.</p>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">The picture below shows the sample data. It=
 has the binary class <em><code>y</code></em> (0 and 1) and two features <e=
m><code>x=E2=82=81</code></em> and <em><code>x=E2=82=82</code></em>.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f1e=
cef" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f1ecef;" de=
coding=3D"async" width=3D"600" height=3D"336" src=3D"https://towardsdatasci=
ence.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1XH3DRC794hXM0EwduNHOSw.png" alt=3D"Sam=
ple for the classification problem (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image-594=
190 has-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Sample for t=
he classification problem (Image by author)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">Our goal is to build a gradient boosting mo=
del that classifies those two classes. The first step is making a uniform p=
rediction on a probability of class 1 (we will call it <em><code>p</code></=
em>) for all the data points. The most reasonable value for the uniform pre=
diction might be the proportion of class 1 which is just a mean of <em><cod=
e>y</code></em>.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f8f=
8f8" data-has-transparency=3D"false" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f8f8f8;" l=
oading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"500" height=3D"62" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/11leQeFha5dLfMjZNG7qtk=
g.png" alt=3D"" class=3D"wp-image-594191 not-transparent"></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">Here is a 3D representation of the data and=
 the initial prediction. At this moment, the prediction is just a plane tha=
t has the uniform value <em><code>p =3D mean(y)</code></em> on the <em><cod=
e>y</code></em> axis all the time.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f7e=
9e6" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f7e9e6;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"600" height=3D"269" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1QMHT83Wo0fRjuanp1HnLf=
Q.png" alt=3D"Prediction plane (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image-594192 =
has-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Prediction plane=
 (Image by author)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">In our data, the mean of <em><code>y</code>=
</em> is 0.56. As it is bigger than 0.5, everything is classified into clas=
s 1 with this initial prediction. Some of you might feel that this uniform =
value prediction does not make sense, but don=E2=80=99t worry. We will impr=
ove our prediction as we add more weak models to it.</p>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">To improve our prediction quality, we might=
 want to focus on the residuals (i.e. prediction error) from our initial pr=
ediction as that is what we want to minimize. The residuals are defined as =
<em><code>r=E1=B5=A2 =3D y=E1=B5=A2 =E2=88=92 p</code></em> (<em><code>i</c=
ode></em> represents the index of each data point). In the figure below, th=
e residuals are shown as the brown lines that are the perpendicular lines f=
rom each data point to the prediction plane.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f3e=
aea" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f3eaea;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"600" height=3D"204" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1P6IX85ExVieosRGGsCRx3=
A.png" alt=3D"Residuals (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image-594193 has-tra=
nsparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Residuals (Image by aut=
hor)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">To minimize these residuals, we are buildin=
g a regression tree model with both <em><code>x=E2=82=81</code></em> and <e=
m><code>x=E2=82=82</code></em> as its features and the residuals <em><code>=
r</code></em> as its target. If we can build a tree that finds some pattern=
s between <em><code>x</code></em> and <em><code>r</code></em>, we can reduc=
e the residuals from the initial prediction <em><code>p</code></em> by util=
izing those found patterns.</p>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">To simplify the demonstration, we are build=
ing very simple trees each of that only has one split and two terminal node=
s which is called "stump". Please note that gradient boosting trees usually=
 have a little deeper trees such as ones with 8 to 32 terminal nodes.</p>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">Here we are creating the first tree predict=
ing the residuals with two different values <em><code>r =3D {0.1, -0.6}</co=
de></em>.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"e1e=
dea" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #e1edea;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1640" height=3D"634" src=3D"http=
s://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1C15KyrZa-m9pr-miVoCX=
tA.png" alt=3D"Created tree (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image-594194 has=
-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Created tree (Image=
 by author)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">You might now think we want to add these pr=
edicted values to our initial prediction <em><code>p</code></em> to reduce =
its residuals if you already read <a href=3D"https://towardsdatascience.com=
/all-you-need-to-know-about-gradient-boosting-algorithm-part-1-regression-2=
520a34a502">the post talking about the regression algorithm</a>, but things=
 are slightly different with classification. The values (we call it <em><co=
de>=CE=B3</code></em> gamma) that we are adding to our initial prediction i=
s computed in the following formula:</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f5f=
7f9" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f5f7f9;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"600" height=3D"156" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/13FWoRhi3Pa_qPjwPtM5v5=
g.png" alt=3D"" class=3D"wp-image-594195 has-transparency"></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">_<code>=CE=A3x=E1=B5=A2=E2=88=88R=E2=B1=BC<=
/code><em>means we are aggregating the values in the sigma </em><code>=CE=
=A3</code><em>on all the sample </em><code>x=E1=B5=A2</code><em>s that belo=
ng to the terminal node </em><code>R=E2=B1=BC</code><em>. </em><code>j</cod=
e><em> represents the index of each terminal node. You might notice that th=
e numerator of the fraction is the sum of the residuals in the terminal nod=
e </em><code>j</code><em>. We will go through all the calculations that giv=
e us this formula in the next section, but let=E2=80=99s just use it to cal=
culate </em><code>=CE=B3</code><em> for now. Below is the computed values o=
f </em><code>=CE=B3=E2=82=81</code><em> and </em><code>=CE=B3=E2=82=82</cod=
e>_.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f7f=
7f7" data-has-transparency=3D"false" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f7f7f7;" l=
oading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"550" height=3D"229" src=3D"http=
s://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1gqvrlNgqFwNCoMhKLaIM=
Eg.png" alt=3D"" class=3D"wp-image-594196 not-transparent"></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">This <em><code>=CE=B3</code></em> is not si=
mply added to our initial prediction <em><code>p</code>.</em> Instead, we a=
re converting <em><code>p</code></em> into log-odds (we will call this log-=
odds converted value <em><code>F(x)</code></em>), then adding <em><code>=CE=
=B3</code></em> to it. For those who are not familiar with log-odds, it is =
defined below. You might have seen it used in <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Logistic_regression#Logistic_model">logistic regression</a>.</p=
>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f7f=
7f7" data-has-transparency=3D"false" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f7f7f7;" l=
oading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"600" height=3D"85" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/14-ChUPnJjLm0DG0ysvBVw=
Q.png" alt=3D"log-odds" class=3D"wp-image-594197 not-transparent"><figcapti=
on class=3D"wp-element-caption">log-odds</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">One more tweak on the prediction update is =
that <em><code>=CE=B3</code></em> is scaled down by <strong>learning rate</=
strong> <em><code>=CE=BD</code></em>, which ranges between 0 and 1, before =
it is added to the log-odds-converted prediction <em><code>F(x)</code></em>=
. This helps the model not to overfit the training data.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f6f=
7f9" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f6f7f9;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"500" height=3D"107" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1Id-ffFq6IFaZ-Gf97Ldpo=
g.png" alt=3D"" class=3D"wp-image-594198 has-transparency"></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">In this example, we use a relatively big le=
arning rate <em><code>=CE=BD =3D 0.9</code></em> to make the optimization p=
rocess easier to understand, but it is usually supposed to be much smaller =
values such as 0.1.</p>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">By substituting actual values for the varia=
bles in the right side of the above equation, we get our updated prediction=
 <em><code>F=E2=82=81(x)</code></em>.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f6f=
6f6" data-has-transparency=3D"false" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f6f6f6;" l=
oading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"700" height=3D"135" src=3D"http=
s://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1k9yIJb8UtPinpijNk8aq=
ZA.png" alt=3D"" class=3D"wp-image-594199 not-transparent"></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">If we convert log-odds <em><code>F(x)</code=
></em> back into the predicted probability <em><code>p(x)</code></em> (we w=
ill cover how we can convert it in the next section), it looks like a stair=
-like object below.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"efe=
2e4" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #efe2e4;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"600" height=3D"293" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1zoKzKYY09WuuuahNKJXlN=
w.png" alt=3D"Updated prediction plane (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image=
-594200 has-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Updated =
prediction plane (Image by author)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">The purple-colored plane is the initial pre=
diction <em><code>p=E2=82=80</code></em> and _<em> it is updated to the red=
 and yellow plane </em><code>p=E2=82=81_</code>.</p>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">Now, the updated residuals <em><code>r</cod=
e></em> looks like this:</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"efe=
2e3" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #efe2e3;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"600" height=3D"263" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1CtZmFFDSKh7pBYpI0BkpK=
w.png" alt=3D"Updated residuals (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image-594201=
 has-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Updated residua=
ls (Image by author)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">In the next step, we are creating a regress=
ion tree again using the same <em><code>x=E2=82=81</code></em> and <em><cod=
e>x=E2=82=82</code></em> as the features and the updated residuals <em><cod=
e>r</code></em> as its target. Here is the created tree:</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"e2e=
cec" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #e2ecec;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"1760" height=3D"530" src=3D"http=
s://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1puVS09-sOyZvmSq6BdND=
hA.png" alt=3D"Created tree (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image-594202 has=
-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Created tree (Image=
 by author)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">We apply the same formula to compute <em><c=
ode>=CE=B3</code></em>. The calculated <em><code>=CE=B3</code></em> along w=
ith the updated prediction <em><code>F=E2=82=82(x)</code></em> are as follo=
ws.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f5f=
6f7" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f5f6f7;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"700" height=3D"218" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1lwNP3R0wEiSOmVdaxJr-Y=
A.png" alt=3D"" class=3D"wp-image-594203 has-transparency"></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">Again, if we convert log-odds <em><code>F=
=E2=82=82(x)</code></em> back into the predicted probability <em><code>p=E2=
=82=82(x)</code></em>, it looks like something below.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"eee=
4e6" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #eee4e6;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"650" height=3D"285" src=3D"https=
://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/1c5yGUZFfHXw7h9jv4eAQV=
g.png" alt=3D"Updated prediction plane (Image by author)" class=3D"wp-image=
-594204 has-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-caption">Updated =
prediction plane (Image by author)</figcaption></figure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">We iterate these steps until the model pred=
iction stops improving. The figures below show the optimization process fro=
m 0 to 4 iterations.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f0e=
ff0" data-has-transparency=3D"true" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f0eff0;" lo=
ading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"742" height=3D"1372" src=3D"http=
s://towardsdatascience.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/15n3NX8MxIA8U8DJerol4=
lQ.png" alt=3D"Prediction update through iterations (Image by author)" clas=
s=3D"wp-image-594205 has-transparency"><figcaption class=3D"wp-element-capt=
ion">Prediction update through iterations (Image by author)</figcaption></f=
igure>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">You can see the combined prediction <em><co=
de>p(x)</code></em> (red and yellow plane) is getting closer to our target =
<em><code>y</code></em> as we add more trees into the combined model<em>.</=
em> This is how gradient boosting works to predict complex targets by combi=
ning multiple weak models.</p>
<p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">The image below summarizes the whole proces=
s of the algorithm.</p>
<figure class=3D"wp-block-image size-large"><img data-dominant-color=3D"f3f=
3f3" data-has-transparency=3D"false" style=3D"--dominant-color: #f3f3f3;" l=
oading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"2138" height=3D"1186" src=3D"ht=
